    "narrative": "Our offensive output today hinges on the prime matchup for **Corbin Carroll**, who leads the squad with an elite 84.80 efficiency score. Facing Janson Junk, **Corbin Carroll** benefits significantly from a favorable SP matchup and a clear platoon advantage, making him our primary engine for both counting stats and the scarce SB category. **Mickey Moniak** also projects as a high-upside play at 75.75, buoyed by a massive 14% park factor boost and a favorable platoon profile against Buehler. While **Josh Naylor** (74.13) and **Seiya Suzuki** (73.90) round out our top-tier starts, **Gabriel Moreno** (69.87) remains a high-floor selection, leveraging a strong 17% SP skill advantage against the vulnerable Janson Junk.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley**, who posted a 55.99 score but ultimately fell short of our UTIL spot occupied by **Vinnie Pasquantino** (65.35). While **Austin Riley** offers pedigree, the combination of a tougher matchup against Gausman and **Vinnie Pasquantino**\u2019s superior dynamic platoon projection rendered the latter the more efficient choice for today\u2019s constraints. We are also monitoring **Griffin Conine** carefully; his 49.55 score is contingent on an assumed #4 spot in the lineup, meaning any pre-game shift in the batting order could necessitate a re-evaluation of his slotting. \n\nRisks are relatively contained today, though we remain cautious with **Jose Caballero** and **Zach McKinstry**. Despite their utility in stealing bases\u2014supported by high-percentage SP delivery windows\u2014their raw scores remain in the mid-40s to 50s due to unfavorable lineup positions (#9 and #8, respectively). We are prioritizing efficiency as we manage game caps, leaving **Trevor Story** (42.25) and **Travis Bazzana** (42.86) on the pine to avoid exposure to high-tier arms like deGrom and Murphy, ensuring our active slots are reserved for hitters with the highest probability of moving the needle in a tigh